[12] Surah pages have no narration system — CONFIRMED, not a "missing files" bug
`grep -l "AudioNarration" surah-*.html` returns zero matches across all 10 surah pages
(surah-fatiha, ayat-al-kursi, and the 9 numbered surahs). This is a feature that does not
exist yet, per charter — correctly not reported as broken.
Assessment of what adding it would take now that Arabic text exists on those pages
(surah-fatiha.html has 99 `data-ar=` attributes, i.e. full bilingual verse/tafsir text is
already authored):
  - Content: EN+AR narration scripts would need authoring per surah (verse-by-verse or
    section-by-section, mirroring the story pattern's {slide, title, script} shape) — text
    exists but is verse/tafsir markup, not narration prose, so this is real writing work, not
    a copy-paste.
  - Data plumbing: add a `surah-<id>` key to both voice-scripts.js and
    scripts/voice-scripts-data.json following the existing schema exactly — the pipeline
    (AudioNarration.init, playAudioFile, playWithWebSpeech fallback) is generic and does not
    need to change, it is keyed purely off the story/surah id string.
  - Audio: 10 surahs x 2 languages via generate-audio-elevenlabs.js — surah pages are shorter
    than story pages (single-page verse presentation, not an 18-slide interactive scene), so
    slide/segment count per surah is likely lower than the story average, but still real
    ElevenLabs quota cost x10.
  - Markup: each surah page needs an `AudioNarration.init('surah-<id>')` call and the narration
    control bar HTML/CSS the story pages already carry (audio-narration.js:195 shows an
    RTL-aware `html[lang="ar"] .narration-control-bar` rule already exists, reusable as-is).
  Net: no architectural blocker — the pipeline is id-driven and RTL-ready — but it is a full
  content+audio production pass across 10 pages, not a small patch.

[13] Bilingual switching vs narration language — MAJOR gap: not reactive
Read audio-narration.js and bilingual.js in full.
  - Language is chosen freshly on every playback START: audio-narration.js:325-330
    `getCurrentLanguage()` returns `window.i18n.getCurrentLanguage()` if present, else
    `localStorage.getItem('islamicKidsLang') || 'en'` — same key bilingual.js writes
    (bilingual.js: `var KEY = 'islamicKidsLang';`). So a fresh play/replay after a language
    toggle DOES pick up the new language correctly — this part works.
  - BUT audio-narration.js only re-evaluates language at two triggers: a `slideChanged`
    DOM event (audio-narration.js:311-315, only when `autoPlayEnabled`) and explicit user
    button clicks (`togglePlayPause` -> `playSlideNarration`, audio-narration.js:459-466).
    It has NO listener for the `languageChanged` event that bilingual.js dispatches on every
    toggle (bilingual.js: `document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('languageChanged', ...))`).
    Confirmed via `grep -n "languageChanged" audio-narration.js` — zero matches.
  - Net effect: if a child/parent taps the language toggle WHILE narration is already playing
    or paused on the current slide, the already-loaded audio track keeps playing in the old
    language; the switch only takes effect on the next natural trigger (next slide's
    auto-narration, or the user manually pressing play/pause again). There is no audio glitch
    or wrong-language mislabeling, but the language toggle is silently inert for in-flight
    narration — a parent who switches to Arabic mid-story and expects the currently-playing
    line to also change will not see that happen until the next slide.
  Fix: add `document.addEventListener('languageChanged', function(e){ ... re-trigger
  playSlideNarration(self.currentSlide, e.detail.lang) ... })` in audio-narration.js's init
  (near line 311, alongside the existing slideChanged listener), guarded to only restart
  playback if audio was actively playing/paused when the toggle fired.

